I believe this may be _theoretically_ unsafe due to inconsistent synchronisation on `pendingFailure`. Consider the following:

```
Thread A                                Thread B

// assume pendingFailure is not null at this point

checkAndScheduleFailure():
    lock (completionStateLock)
    {
        if (pendingFailure == null)
            // fails
    }
                                        checkAndScheduleFailure():
                                            lock (completionStateLock)
                                            {
                                                if (pendingFailure == null)
                                                    // fails
                                            }

// both threads now run rest of method
// depending on timing, they either both execute/schedule pendingFailure,
// or one of them gets a (potentially delayed) nullref
```

This would be a pretty rare scenario, but potentially not impossible (something like cancelling a request that also happens to naturally fail at roughly the same time?)

All other reads/writes of `pendingFailure` should probably take the lock, too. As for this particular method, writing out the reference to `pendingFailure` to a local under the lock and then using that copy outside (instead of the field directly) would probably work fine?
